{"step_index":3468,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-22T18:30:49Z","thinking":"The API call receives cases in a specific JSON format, each detailing a request with potential matches. The expected output is also JSON, specifying match status and a corresponding item ID, along with a reason. Analyzing the user's last statement reveals the need to understand how to proceed.\n\n\nInitially, the strategy was to send all cases in a single request. However, the server rejected large requests, leading to an iterative approach of splitting the cases into smaller batches, first in halves, then further, until four batches. It became evident that this splitting would quickly consume the daily message quota. Examining the output limits of the system indicates the core issue is likely the response size exceeding the token limit, leading to truncated JSON responses. The batch failure is likely due to the system returning non-JSON content.\n\n\nThe core problem is likely unnecessary output bloat due to including 'no_match' cases, exceeding token limits. Optimizing by only returning 'MATCH' or 'REVIEW' significantly reduces output size, enabling single-request processing.  This dramatically reduces token usage and request count, and avoids exceeding ChatGPT's message limits. The user's request for single maximum-size requests likely confirms the large response problem.\n\n\nAnalyzing user instructions clarifies an adaptive batching strategy is desired: prioritize single-batch submission, recursively subdividing on failure. Reducing the output to essential match/review data is crucial to stay within token limits.\n\nThe user's description of a similar successful PDF analysis suggests the feasibility of this approach. Reviewing the existing AI engineer folder confirms the availability of a relevant endpoint optimized for ChatGPT, and will likely improve performance.\n\n\n\nFocus shifts to implementing a specialized \"MSE max\" approach, potentially leveraging Gemini AI Studio. The existing system needs modification to extract only item names, excluding prices or tables, to reduce output size and potentially avoid token limits. This adapted approach aims to send a single batch request, recursively splitting if the batch fails. Analysis includes reviewing logs and adapting the code for batch processing.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"find_by_name","args":{"MaxDepth":2,"Pattern":"*mse*","SearchDirectory":"d:\\AI_Engineer","toolAction":"Find MSE folders","toolSummary":"Search for MSEMAX directory in d:\\AI_Engineer"}}]}
